<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/9th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Campaign</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hungarian invasions of Europe</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 899–970</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Hungarian raids</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Hungarian invasions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> were the raids made by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Magyars</a> from the Carpathian Basin into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> between about 899 and 970. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mounted-archer">Mounted archers</a> struck as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bremen">Bremen</a>, southern France and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>. The raids were checked by Otto I's victory over the Hungarians at the Lechfeld in 955 and ended after a Byzantine victory at Arcadiopolis in 970, after which the Hungarians formed a settled Christian kingdom.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hungarian-invasions-of-europe.jpg" alt="Hungarian invasions of Europe" width="320" height="219" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Csanády · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kalandozasok.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The raids came at a time when the lands of the former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire</a> were threatened from three sides: by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Magyars</a> from the east, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> expansion from the north and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> from the south. Before they settled in the Carpathian Basin the Hungarians already fought as allies and hired troops of their neighbours. They took part in the wars of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-bulgarian-empire">First Bulgarian Empire</a>, raided the East Slavic tribes and sold captives to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerch">Kerch</a>, and joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arnulf-of-carinthia">Arnulf of Carinthia</a> against Great Moravia in 892. Driven west by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgarians">Bulgarians</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pechenegs">Pechenegs</a> in 895, they occupied the basin around 896, probably under the leadership of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arpad">Árpád</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In 899 the Hungarians defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berengar-i-of-italy">Berengar I of Italy</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brenta">Brenta</a> and plundered the north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. Between 907 and 910 they destroyed three large East Frankish armies: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavarians">Bavarians</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bratislava">Pressburg</a> in 907, Burchard, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a>, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eisenach">Eisenach</a> in 908, and the army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-child">Louis the Child</a> at the first Battle of Lechfeld in 910. In the following decades their horsemen burned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bremen">Bremen</a>, sacked Basel, crossed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> to the Atlantic, raided Catalonia in 942 and reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apulia">Apulia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otranto">Otranto</a> in the south of Italy.</p><p>Their armies were mostly light cavalry. They struck without warning, plundered and left before a defence could gather; when forced to fight they shot arrows and pretended to flee, drawing their enemies into broken pursuit. Rulers often bought peace with tribute, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-fowler">Henry the Fowler</a> did in 924 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> did after 934. Historians count 45 or 47 raids between 899 and 970, of which only eight failed. A hymn from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modena">Modena</a> around 900 asks to be saved from the arrows of the Hungarians.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The defeat by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-i">Otto I</a> at Lechfeld on 10 August 955, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Magyars</a> lost about 5,000 warriors, ended the raids on the west; the captured leaders Bulcsú, Lehel and Sur were hanged at Regensburg. The defeat helped bring about the revival of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> in 962. Raids on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> went on until 970, when Hungarian auxiliaries fought with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sviatoslav-i-of-kiev">Sviatoslav I of Kiev</a> at Arcadiopolis. The Hungarians were the last invading people to settle for good in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-europe">Central Europe</a>, and in the following centuries they adopted western forms of feudal warfare, above all heavily armoured cavalry.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hungarian invasions of Europe” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Hungarian raids</b> from the 890s weakened Frankish control.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-pannonian-croatia">Duchy of Pannonian Croatia</a></li><li><blockquote>He had defeated <b>Hungarian raids</b> and the army of Simeon I of Bulgaria, uniting the Croats in one kingdom.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-croatia-925-1102">Kingdom of Croatia (925-1102)</a></li><li><blockquote>Berengar of Friuli was elected King of Italy in 888 and crowned emperor in 915, while <b>Hungarian raids</b> devastated the region.</blockquote><a href="/state/march-of-friuli">March of Friuli</a></li></ul></section>
